WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:03.567
-Our first guest tonight is
an Academy Award-winning

00:00:03.567 --> 00:00:05.400
writer and actor
you know from films such as

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:06.934
"Good Will Hunting,"
"The Martian,"

00:00:06.934 --> 00:00:08.167
and "Ford v. Ferrari."

00:00:08.167 --> 00:00:10.234
He both produces
and stars in "Air,"

00:00:10.234 --> 00:00:11.601
which is in theaters now.

00:00:11.601 --> 00:00:12.868
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.868 --> 00:00:15.767
-I'm willing to bet
my career on Michael Jordan.

00:00:15.767 --> 00:00:17.501
-Come on, man.

00:00:17.501 --> 00:00:19.934
-You asked me what I do here.
This is what I do.

00:00:19.934 --> 00:00:23.100
I find you players,
and I [bleep] feel it this time.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.100
Okay, it's risky.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:26.434
When you were selling sneakers
out of the back of

00:00:26.434 --> 00:00:28.000
your Plymouth, that was risky.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.100
It took balls.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.467
I mean, that's why
we're all here.

00:00:30.467 --> 00:00:31.801
Don't change that now.

00:00:31.801 --> 00:00:33.767
Phil, I mean,
if you look at him,

00:00:33.767 --> 00:00:36.234
if you really look at Jordan
like I did,

00:00:36.234 --> 00:00:37.968
you're going to see
exactly what I see.

00:00:37.968 --> 00:00:39.467
-Which is what?

00:00:39.467 --> 00:00:42.167
-The most competitive guy
I have ever seen.

00:00:42.167 --> 00:00:43.834
He is a [bleep] killer.

00:00:43.834 --> 00:00:46.701
-Please welcome to the show
Matt Damon, everybody.

00:00:46.701 --> 00:00:49.367
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:01:06.367 --> 00:01:09.000
I'm so happy you're here.
-It's good to be here.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.901
-How are you?
-I'm doing really well.

00:01:10.901 --> 00:01:12.601
I really enjoyed this film.

00:01:12.601 --> 00:01:14.100
It looked like it was
a lot of fun to make.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.667
Obviously, you're collaborating
with people

00:01:15.667 --> 00:01:16.968
you've been working
with over the years.

00:01:16.968 --> 00:01:18.868
And a really nice wardrobe
for you.

00:01:18.868 --> 00:01:20.734
Just a solid '80s wardrobe.

00:01:20.734 --> 00:01:22.901
-Yeah, I got a
shout-out to Charlese,

00:01:22.901 --> 00:01:23.901
our costume designer.

00:01:23.901 --> 00:01:25.334
She crushed it.

00:01:25.334 --> 00:01:27.734
In fact, the guy that I play
is a real guy, Sonny Vaccaro.

00:01:27.734 --> 00:01:31.267
And when Sonny came to the set
to visit with his wife,

00:01:31.267 --> 00:01:33.300
she looked at what I was wearing
and she turned to Charlese,

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.200
our costume designer, and said,

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:39.334
"It's like you
broke into his closet."

00:01:39.334 --> 00:01:41.367
So that's about the
highest praise you can get.

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:44.734
-Absolutely. Now, I guess, like,
not a lot of '80s clothes

00:01:44.734 --> 00:01:46.300
were cool,
but Sonny was definitely

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:47.901
not wearing the coolest
of the era.

00:01:47.901 --> 00:01:49.300
-No.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:50.701
-I don't feel like anybody's
going to watch this movie --

00:01:50.701 --> 00:01:52.834
None of the teens are going
to watch this movie and go like,

00:01:52.834 --> 00:01:55.634
"I want to dress like Matt Damon
in this movie."

00:01:55.634 --> 00:01:57.968
-No. Like, there's those things
that you can get,

00:01:57.968 --> 00:02:00.767
like the rider in your contract
where you keep the wardrobe.

00:02:00.767 --> 00:02:03.267
Like, this wasn't
one of those jobs.

00:02:03.267 --> 00:02:04.334
-Your rider was, "I never want
to see it again."

00:02:04.334 --> 00:02:05.901
-Please.
-A lot of beige.

00:02:05.901 --> 00:02:07.868
I think you found out
if beige was your color.

00:02:07.868 --> 00:02:11.734
-Yeah. We found out that
it's definitely not my color.

00:02:11.734 --> 00:02:13.734
But, actually, yeah,
when I showed

00:02:13.734 --> 00:02:15.267
a rough cut of the movie
to my wife

00:02:15.267 --> 00:02:18.534
and the movie ended,
and I turned it on and I said,

00:02:18.534 --> 00:02:19.701
"What'd you think?"

00:02:19.701 --> 00:02:24.000
She goes, "That movie's great.
You look awful."

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.300
Yeah.
-Very nice.

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:26.834
At least you started
with praise.

00:02:26.834 --> 00:02:28.434
-Just two really true
declarative statements.

00:02:28.434 --> 00:02:29.601
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:29.601 --> 00:02:31.467
-So, this is based on
the team of people,

00:02:31.467 --> 00:02:33.701
you know, behind the scenes
who are putting together

00:02:33.701 --> 00:02:36.200
the deal for Michael Jordan
to come to Nike,

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:40.868
which changed, obviously, the
future of of basketball shoes.

00:02:40.868 --> 00:02:41.901
And I guess it was inspired --

00:02:41.901 --> 00:02:43.334
Maybe the writer came up
with the idea

00:02:43.334 --> 00:02:45.334
of watching ""The Last Dance,"
which I think everybody did

00:02:45.334 --> 00:02:46.601
during those early days
of the pandemic.

00:02:46.601 --> 00:02:47.968
-Yeah, yeah.

00:02:47.968 --> 00:02:51.033
He -- Alex, the writer,
I think was inspired by that.

00:02:51.033 --> 00:02:52.501
And, I mean, we all, you know,
sat and watched

00:02:52.501 --> 00:02:53.634
"The Last Dance."

00:02:53.634 --> 00:02:55.300
I think that's how
we all spent the first --

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:57.367
-I would have watched it anyway,
but it certainly showed up

00:02:57.367 --> 00:02:58.467
at the right time.

00:02:58.467 --> 00:03:00.701
-I think I watched it
a lot faster.

00:03:00.701 --> 00:03:02.033
-Yeah. I will say this.

00:03:02.033 --> 00:03:04.367
I don't think my wife
would have watched it with me

00:03:04.367 --> 00:03:05.868
unless it was a pandemic.

00:03:05.868 --> 00:03:08.901
-My wife, my kids, like --
Yeah, yeah, exactly.

00:03:08.901 --> 00:03:11.968
My COVID bubble.
We all -- We binged it together.

00:03:11.968 --> 00:03:14.067
-In the COVID bubble,
did you try to --

00:03:14.067 --> 00:03:15.367
I mean, first of all,

00:03:15.367 --> 00:03:17.667
I don't know how much
your kids engage with your work.

00:03:17.667 --> 00:03:19.601
Do they like watching you?

00:03:19.601 --> 00:03:21.067
-Uh...no.

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:24.234
-Yeah. Did you take advantage
of the COVID bubble to say,

00:03:24.234 --> 00:03:26.968
"You know, let's go revisit
some of dad's early work?"

00:03:26.968 --> 00:03:29.434
-Absolutely not.
-No, they wouldn't stand for it?

00:03:29.434 --> 00:03:31.467
-No, I have --
Well, my my 16-year-old

00:03:31.467 --> 00:03:35.267
likes to give me a hard time,
and she is very clear

00:03:35.267 --> 00:03:37.534
about not wanting to
see anything

00:03:37.534 --> 00:03:39.300
that I'm in
if she thinks it might be good.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:40.367
-Oh, interesting.
-Yeah.

00:03:40.367 --> 00:03:42.067
So she'll
watch anything she hears --

00:03:42.067 --> 00:03:44.200
Like, if I get bad reviews
in something,

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:46.434
she's like -- That's
the one she wants to see.

00:03:46.434 --> 00:03:47.567
-Oh, my God.
-Yeah.

00:03:47.567 --> 00:03:49.901
-That is the most ice-cold
16-year-old thing

00:03:49.901 --> 00:03:51.133
I've ever heard.

00:03:51.133 --> 00:03:55.100
-There's one movie
I did that's not very good,

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:56.801
and it's called
"The Great Wall."

00:03:56.801 --> 00:04:01.067
And my daughter was talking
to one of her friends

00:04:01.067 --> 00:04:02.133
and she was talking
about this movie.

00:04:02.133 --> 00:04:03.567
"My dad did this movie
'The Wall.'"

00:04:03.567 --> 00:04:04.634
And she kept
calling it "The Wall."

00:04:04.634 --> 00:04:05.934
And I was finally like,

00:04:05.934 --> 00:04:07.501
"Isabella, that movie is called
'The Great Wall.'"

00:04:07.501 --> 00:04:10.601
And she was like, "Dad, there is
nothing great about that movie."

00:04:10.601 --> 00:04:11.868
[ Applause ]

00:04:11.868 --> 00:04:14.000
-Okay.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:15.934
-I like thinking
that she knew for months,

00:04:15.934 --> 00:04:17.234
"I'm going to just keep
calling it 'The Wall.'"

00:04:17.234 --> 00:04:19.334
just setting you up,
setting you up.

00:04:19.334 --> 00:04:22.033
-She's like --
Yeah, like, just like this

00:04:22.033 --> 00:04:26.167
for months until I'm like,
"It's not called 'The Wall.'"

00:04:26.167 --> 00:04:27.667
Yeah.

00:04:27.667 --> 00:04:31.000
-The old-school boxy car phone.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.067
That was a really nice thing
to see in this film.

00:04:33.067 --> 00:04:34.267
Do you remember
the first time you saw

00:04:34.267 --> 00:04:36.133
someone on the car phone?

00:04:36.133 --> 00:04:38.767
-Yeah. In fact,
we put this line in there like,

00:04:38.767 --> 00:04:41.534
"I'm calling you on the --
There's a phone in the car."

00:04:41.534 --> 00:04:42.934
Like, he's really
excited about it,

00:04:42.934 --> 00:04:45.434
because we remembered when --
you know, in the '80s,

00:04:45.434 --> 00:04:48.000
when we first saw a car phone,
and it was like, "Wow!

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.567
Like, that car
has a phone in it."

00:04:49.567 --> 00:04:51.133
It's like the craziest idea.

00:04:51.133 --> 00:04:53.834
-I think most of those
early calls were just people

00:04:53.834 --> 00:04:55.534
telling them they were
calling them from a car.

00:04:55.534 --> 00:04:56.801
-You know what?

00:04:56.801 --> 00:05:00.400
Actually, Ben and I
had a roommate for a summer

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:05.601
in California in 19--
I want to say '90, '91, maybe.

00:05:05.601 --> 00:05:09.234
And this kid,
he couldn't afford a car phone.

00:05:09.234 --> 00:05:13.367
He bought a plastic phone.
This is a true story.

00:05:13.367 --> 00:05:15.467
Bought a plastic phone
and would --

00:05:15.467 --> 00:05:19.467
at red lights,
would put it up to his ear

00:05:19.467 --> 00:05:22.434
with, like,
a cord going to nothing.

00:05:22.434 --> 00:05:24.634
And that was his attempt
to try to, like,

00:05:24.634 --> 00:05:27.901
pick up women,
like, at stoplights,

00:05:27.901 --> 00:05:29.434
because it was
such a rare thing.

00:05:29.434 --> 00:05:31.834
-I mean, it definitely seems
like he didn't plan for whatever

00:05:31.834 --> 00:05:34.701
Step 2 was.

00:05:34.701 --> 00:05:36.300
-No. Like, can you imagine?

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:38.567
"Can I borrow your phone
for a second?

00:05:38.567 --> 00:05:39.734
I got to make a call."

00:05:39.734 --> 00:05:40.734
-"Oh, no!"
-Yeah.

00:05:40.734 --> 00:05:43.434
"I'm out. I'm out. I'm out."

00:05:43.434 --> 00:05:47.267
-"You ask too many questions."
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:05:47.267 --> 00:05:50.534
-We had a your co-star
Jason Bateman here,

00:05:50.534 --> 00:05:52.033
and we were talking
about the '80s.

00:05:52.033 --> 00:05:55.300
And Jason was admitting that he
kind of crushed the '80s.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:57.100
Like, he had
a really good decade.

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:59.267
You were mentioning L.A. with
Ben in the early '90s.

00:05:59.267 --> 00:06:00.601
Obviously, you know, the '90s

00:06:00.601 --> 00:06:02.467
eventually worked out
pretty well for you guys.

00:06:02.467 --> 00:06:05.367
But how were the '80s and '90s
for you as far as

00:06:05.367 --> 00:06:07.400
fashion, hair,
just a general vibe?

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:10.467
-We did not crush the 80s?
-No, you didn't? Yeah.

00:06:10.467 --> 00:06:14.000
-We did not.
We did not crush the '80s.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:16.501
Up to the mid-'90s,
we were struggling.

00:06:16.501 --> 00:06:17.968
Yeah, we weren't
crushing anything.

00:06:17.968 --> 00:06:20.567
Bateman was dominating.
That's true. Yeah.

00:06:20.567 --> 00:06:23.200
Yeah, Bateman -- I can see how
he loved those years.

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:24.467
We were not.

00:06:24.467 --> 00:06:25.801
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:25.801 --> 00:06:28.100
Do you feel like --
Do you ever look back --

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:30.667
I have this, I think,
very false sense of myself

00:06:30.667 --> 00:06:33.300
through the '80s,
which is, I don't think

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:36.100
I wore the worst fashion,
forgetting that

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:39.901
even like a middle-tier
bad choice looks awful now.

00:06:39.901 --> 00:06:42.934
-Yeah. I don't think I wore
anything that looked good.

00:06:42.934 --> 00:06:45.968
Like, I haven't seen
a good picture of me in the '80s

00:06:45.968 --> 00:06:49.200
or really, you know,
until about 1996.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:51.901
Like, until people start--
professional people

00:06:51.901 --> 00:06:53.033
started dressing me for movies.

00:06:53.033 --> 00:06:54.133
-Yeah, right.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:06:54.133 --> 00:06:55.234
-Like, someone who knows
what they're doing

00:06:55.234 --> 00:06:57.334
comes over with a comb
and is like,

00:06:57.334 --> 00:07:00.133
"I'm about to fix everything."

00:07:00.133 --> 00:07:02.968
-"You need to put this on and
you need to ask no questions."

00:07:02.968 --> 00:07:04.067
And -- Yeah.

00:07:04.067 --> 00:07:05.801
-I feel like every
almost-good picture

00:07:05.801 --> 00:07:09.701
I see of myself in that era,
like, one thing will ruin it.

00:07:09.701 --> 00:07:12.234
It's like,
"Oh, hardcore pleated pants."

00:07:12.234 --> 00:07:13.734
-Right.
-Just like...

00:07:13.734 --> 00:07:15.167
-Yeah. Oh, my God. Yeah.

00:07:15.167 --> 00:07:16.934
I'm thinking back
to my prom picture.

00:07:16.934 --> 00:07:18.467
Yeah, there's not --
I don't think I --

00:07:18.467 --> 00:07:20.234
-It wasn't a good era.
We didn't grow up in a good era.

00:07:20.234 --> 00:07:21.634
-It was not strong.

00:07:21.634 --> 00:07:22.801
It was not -- But I feel like we

00:07:22.801 --> 00:07:23.834
represented it well
in the movie.

00:07:23.834 --> 00:07:25.200
-You really did.

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:27.534
I should stress, you --
And it was a lot of nostalgia.

00:07:27.534 --> 00:07:30.234
Obviously, we both --
we're Boston sports fans.

00:07:30.234 --> 00:07:33.534
M.J. is someone that you could
watch and have appreciation for,

00:07:33.534 --> 00:07:36.067
but at the same time,
you know, he --

00:07:36.067 --> 00:07:39.267
You know, I was rooting against
him for most of his career.

00:07:39.267 --> 00:07:41.100
-Yeah.
-Did you see him play live?

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:42.234
-I did, yeah.

00:07:42.234 --> 00:07:45.567
I did because my dad had tickets
through his work

00:07:45.567 --> 00:07:47.601
that we could go
to one game a year.

00:07:47.601 --> 00:07:49.601
And. obviously,
Boston in the '80s,

00:07:49.601 --> 00:07:51.167
it was Celtics-Lakers that --

00:07:51.167 --> 00:07:53.200
So Lakers tickets
were not offered.

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:55.968
They were off the table.
That was those were for clients.

00:07:55.968 --> 00:07:58.133
They were like,
"Any other game you can go to."

00:07:58.133 --> 00:08:00.834
And so my brother and I
would always want to go see M.J.

00:08:00.834 --> 00:08:03.434
just to say that we saw him
live, because it was --

00:08:03.434 --> 00:08:05.968
You know, I mean, he just
kind of transcended the game.

00:08:05.968 --> 00:08:08.701
He was -- It was
just so exciting to watch him.

00:08:08.701 --> 00:08:10.434
And then, of course,
like, they became kind of

00:08:10.434 --> 00:08:12.234
America's team
because the national game

00:08:12.234 --> 00:08:14.334
every week was the Bulls.

00:08:14.334 --> 00:08:17.033
And, you know, he was just
kind of bigger than the game.

00:08:17.033 --> 00:08:18.400
-I mentioned
we were Red Sox fans.

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:19.734
You were very kind.

00:08:19.734 --> 00:08:21.934
This was, I think, the beginning
of my second season.

00:08:21.934 --> 00:08:24.133
You hosted the season
premiere of "SNL."

00:08:24.133 --> 00:08:25.567
You were kind enough
to do something.

00:08:25.567 --> 00:08:28.801
Look at -- We are the same.
We did not age.

00:08:28.801 --> 00:08:30.567
We did not age at all.

00:08:30.567 --> 00:08:34.133
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Wow!

00:08:34.133 --> 00:08:35.400
Wow!
-You know what?

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:37.501
Do you remember,
in the beginning of

00:08:37.501 --> 00:08:38.667
the "Captain America" movie,

00:08:38.667 --> 00:08:41.300
they, like, -- They use,
like, digital effects

00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:43.667
to make Chris Evans look like
a smaller version of himself?

00:08:43.667 --> 00:08:47.133
I feel like I'm
the smaller version here.

00:08:47.133 --> 00:08:50.200
That's, like -- That's before
the super serum.

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:51.400
-That's amazing.

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:52.801
-Do you -- That was --

00:08:52.801 --> 00:08:55.701
So, that's 2002 that you hosted.
-Yeah.

00:08:55.701 --> 00:08:58.467
-Do you remember, like, getting
the call to be asked to do that?

00:08:58.467 --> 00:08:59.701
I mean, obviously, you're pretty
established in your career

00:08:59.701 --> 00:09:00.834
at that point.

00:09:00.834 --> 00:09:02.100
-Yeah. You know,
what was funny about that

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:05.400
was I actually --
My agent, Patrick,

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:06.968
who's been my agent for
30 years,

00:09:06.968 --> 00:09:08.267
called me up and goes,

00:09:08.267 --> 00:09:10.400
"You're hosting
'Saturday Night Live.'

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:11.701
You know,
it's the season opener."

00:09:11.701 --> 00:09:13.100
And I was like, "Yeah, great."

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:14.934
I was like,
"But I'm not promoting anything.

00:09:14.934 --> 00:09:15.934
I don't have any --"
And he goes,

00:09:15.934 --> 00:09:17.000
"No, no, no, of course."

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:18.200
And he goes,
"Bruce Springsteen's

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:19.801
the musical guest."

00:09:19.801 --> 00:09:21.434
So I was like,
"Oh, yeah, of course."

00:09:21.434 --> 00:09:24.000
So, like, we're big fans
of Springsteen.

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:26.834
So, like, just we --
I basically hosted the show

00:09:26.834 --> 00:09:30.567
so that I could go
watch him do the two songs.

00:09:30.567 --> 00:09:32.400
-Not a bad play.
-No, not at all.

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:34.434
I had a great time.
I really -- I loved it.

00:09:34.434 --> 00:09:38.534
But I didn't host it
for another 16 years after that.

00:09:38.534 --> 00:09:40.701
